from pathlib import Path
import json
H=Path(__file__).resolve().parent
entries=[]
def source(date,needle):
    fallback=None
    for p in sorted(H.glob('root*.json')):
        for i,r in enumerate(json.loads(p.read_text())):
            text='\n'.join(c.get('text','') for c in r.get('result',{}).get('content',[]) if c.get('type')=='text')
            for line in text.splitlines():
                if (not date or date in line) and needle.lower() in line.lower():return line,f'{p.name}[{i}]'
                if date and date in line and fallback is None:fallback=(line,f'{p.name}[{i}]')
    if fallback:return fallback
    raise ValueError((date,needle))
def E(date,needle,evidence,field,title,question,summary,defs,statement,proof,setup,calc,conclusion,limits,extension,refs=()):
    id=f'Y24-06-{len(entries)+1:03d}';excerpt,receipt=source(date,needle)
    entries.append(dict(id=id,title=title,status='Classical mathematics with newly authored derivation or finite example and explicit scope',summary=summary,definitions=defs,result=dict(name=title,statement=statement,proof=proof),example=dict(setup=setup,calculation=calc,conclusion=conclusion),limits=limits,extension=extension,references=[dict(label=a,url=b) for a,b in refs],original=dict(date=date,month='2024-06',evidence=evidence,field=field,concept=title,origin='Recovered dated mathematical subject; historical speaker and wording limits are retained below.',question=question,math=statement,finish=conclusion,kind='Developed proof or worked example',refs=[],source_excerpt=excerpt,retrieval_receipt=receipt,original_question=None,attribution_note=('The search attributes this topic to a user question, but does not supply a verbatim full prompt. The revised formulation is new.' if evidence=='U' else 'This is an assistant passage or topic fragment, not a user-authored mathematical claim. The revised formulation and development are new.'))))

E('2024-06-02','Number systems','A','Algebra','A number-system catalogue organized by actual algebraic properties',
 'Which listed objects are fields, ordered fields, noncommutative division algebras or nonassociative structures, and which properties fail in each extension?',
 'The historical list ranged from natural numbers through finite fields, p-adics, hyperreals and nonassociative algebras. A rigorous treatment cannot place all of them in one inclusion chain. Small constructions show which axioms distinguish them and why algebraic dimension is not physical space.',
 ['The field Q(√2) consists of a+b√2 with a,b∈Q. The finite field F₄ is F₂[t]/(t²+t+1).', 'Quaternion units satisfy i²=j²=k²=−1 and ij=k=−ji. An ordered field must have every nonzero square positive.'],
 'Q(√2) is a two-dimensional Q-vector-space field; F₄ has four elements and characteristic two; the quaternions are a four-dimensional real division algebra but not a commutative field. No ordering compatible with field operations can make C an ordered field.',
 'For a+b√2≠0, its product with a−b√2 is a²−2b², a nonzero rational number because √2 is irrational. This gives an explicit inverse and proves field closure. Over F₂, t²+t+1 has no root, hence is irreducible; the quotient is a field. If α is its class, α²=α+1 and α(α+1)=1. In quaternions ij≠ji, so the commutative field axiom fails even though nonzero elements have inverses. Finally, an ordered field has 1>0 and a²≥0; a square root i of −1 would force −1≥0, contradicting the order.',
 'Compare the meanings of the numeral two in Q(√2), F₄ and H.',
 'In Q(√2), two is invertible with inverse1/2. In F₄, two equals zero because 1+1=0, although α is neither zero nor one. In H, real two is central and invertible, while products of imaginary units depend on order. These differences concern operations and axioms, not the number of coordinates used to display a numeral.',
 'A table of structures becomes meaningful when its inclusions, scalar fields and failed axioms are explicitly named.',
 'The recovered list did not provide constructions of all hyperreal, surreal, ordinal or cardinal systems. This entry does not conflate those distinct objects or claim they are all field extensions of R.',
 'For any two proposed systems, specify whether the comparison is a field embedding, an ordered embedding, a vector-space identification or merely a set injection.',
 [('J S Milne Fields and Galois Theory','https://jmilne.org/math/CourseNotes/FT.pdf'),('John Baez The Octonions','https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0105155')])

E('2024-06-05','Cyclic','A','Lie theory','Lie-bracket closure does not imply cyclic time evolution',
 'Which matrices generate the claimed Lie algebra, and which actual flow has periodic rather than expanding trajectories?',
 'The cyclic-universe passage supplied brackets, derived algebras and invariant subalgebras but no model whose periodicity followed from them. Matrix examples separate closure and solvability from the spectral condition that produces a cyclic orbit.',
 ['For matrices use [A,B]=AB−BA and the derived algebra g′=span of all brackets.', 'Set E=[[0,1],[0,0]], F=[[0,0],[1,0]], H=diag(1,−1), and J=E−F.'],
 'The relations [H,E]=2E, [H,F]=−2F and [E,F]=H generate sl₂. Its derived algebra is itself. Nevertheless exp(tJ)=Icos t+Jsin t is periodic, while exp(tH)=diag(e^t,e^(−t)) is not periodic except on the zero state.',
 'Direct multiplication gives the three bracket relations and shows that every basis generator lies in the bracket span, proving g′=g. Since J²=−I, its exponential series separates into even and odd powers and gives the trigonometric formula. Since H is diagonal, its exponential has the displayed expanding and contracting factors. A nonzero vector fixed after positive time under exp(tH) would require one of e^t or e^(−t) to equal one, impossible for positive t. The algebra containing both generators therefore does not force either of their one-parameter subgroups to share the other’s behavior.',
 'Compare initial vector (1,0) under the two generators.',
 'Under J the path is (cos t,−sin t), has Euclidean norm one and period2π. Under H it is (e^t,0), with exponentially changing norm. Also span{H,E} is a closed two-dimensional subalgebra with derived algebra span{E} and next derived algebra zero, whereas adding F yields the nonsolvable sl₂ algebra. These are exact algebraic distinctions independent of any cosmological labels.',
 'A claim about cycles needs a specified vector field, initial state and periodicity calculation. Bracket closure is a different theorem.',
 'No expansion/contraction history or physical cyclic-universe model is established. The historical higher-dimensional brackets were not returned in enough detail to reconstruct their structure constants.',
 'Given proposed structure constants, verify antisymmetry and Jacobi first; then analyze the flow of a separately declared generator or Hamiltonian.',
 [('Meinrenken Lie groups and Lie algebras','https://www.math.toronto.edu/mein/teaching/LectureNotes/lie.pdf')])

E('2024-06-05','symplectic','U','Symplectic geometry','Hamiltonian conservation is not the Casimir property',
 'Which symplectic form and Poisson bracket are intended, and is the proposed quantity conserved by one flow or by every Hamiltonian flow?',
 'The recovered user topic asked for correctness and numerics concerning symplectic phase space, sp(2n), commutators and Casimirs. A canonical oscillator provides a complete calculation and exposes a particularly important distinction: on a connected nondegenerate canonical phase space, a Casimir is constant.',
 ['Use coordinates (x₁,x₂,p₁,p₂), J=[[0,I],[−I,0]] and the canonical bracket {f,g}=Σi(f_xi g_pi−f_pi g_xi).', 'A Casimir C satisfies {C,f}=0 for every smooth f. The symplectic Lie algebra is sp(4,R)={A:AᵀJ+JA=0}.'],
 'For H=(|x|²+|p|²)/2 the flow is ż=Jz, preserves H and the symplectic form, and has period2π. It is not true that this nonconstant H is a canonical Casimir. Every differentiable canonical Casimir on a connected domain is constant.',
 'The skew matrix J has J²=−I, giving exp(tJ)=Icos t+Jsin t. Its transpose identity exp(tJ)ᵀJexp(tJ)=J follows either from multiplication or differentiating it and using AᵀJ+JA=0. Energy preservation follows from Ḣ=(∇H)ᵀJ∇H=0 because J is skew. For the Casimir claim, use test functions x_i and p_i: {C,x_i}=−C_pi=0 and {C,p_i}=C_xi=0. Thus every first derivative is zero, and connectedness gives constancy. The equality {H,H}=0 only proves conservation under H’s own flow.',
 'Take x₁=cos t, p₁=−sin t, x₂=sin t, p₂=cos t.',
 'These functions satisfy Hamilton’s equations exactly, have H=1 and angular momentum x₁p₂−x₂p₁=1. But {H,x₁}=−p₁ is not identically zero, so H fails the Casimir test. In quantization, [x_i,p_j]=iℏδijI belongs to an operator algebra; it must not be substituted for the classical Poisson relation {x_i,p_j}=δij without changing the mathematical setting.',
 'The historical oscillator is a valid Hamiltonian example; its existence is not a universal model of flight, and its conserved Hamiltonian is not a universal commutator invariant.',
 'Nonconstant Casimirs can occur for degenerate Poisson structures, including Lie–Poisson structures on dual Lie algebras. That is a different space from this canonical symplectic domain.',
 'For an explicit degenerate Poisson tensor, solve J(z)∇C=0 and compare its genuine Casimirs with first integrals of one chosen Hamiltonian.',
 [('Meinrenken Introduction to Poisson Geometry','https://www.math.toronto.edu/mein/teaching/MAT1341_PoissonGeometry/Poisson7.pdf')])

E('2024-06-05','rationals','U','Geometry and arithmetic','Rational coordinates do not select an ambient dimension',
 'How do cardinality, scalar-field dimension, intrinsic topology and ambient placement differ in the proposed number sheets?',
 'The user asked whether different kinds of numbers could exist on two- or three-dimensional sheets. A coordinate embedding can place the same numbers in many ambient spaces without changing their algebra. Rational points on a circle give a stronger example: exact rational coordinates can lie densely on a curved geometric object.',
 ['Q² is the set of ordered pairs of rationals; Q⊂R can also be placed in R³ by q↦(q,0,0). These are different choices of ambient coordinates.', 'For rational t set P(t)=((1−t²)/(1+t²),2t/(1+t²)).'],
 'P(t) is a rational point on x²+y²=1. Every rational point except (−1,0) arises this way. Q² is countable and dense in R², while R is uncountable; none of these facts makes rationals inherently two-dimensional or reals inherently three-dimensional.',
 'Squaring and adding the coordinates of P(t) gives [(1−t²)²+4t²]/(1+t²)²=1. Conversely a rational point with x≠−1 has t=y/(1+x), and substitution using x²+y²=1 recovers the two coordinates. Rational t values are dense in R, and continuity of P makes these rational points dense in the circle except for the limiting missing point, which is approached as |t| grows. Countability of Q² follows by enumerating pairs of a countable set, while density follows by choosing a rational number in each coordinate interval. A set injection into an ambient space makes no claim about linear or topological equivalence.',
 'Take t=3/4.',
 'The circle point is (7/25,24/25), with squared coordinates summing to one. The same pair can be displayed as (7/25,24/25,0) in R³, and the rational number 7/25 can be displayed on a line in either R² or R³. Its rationality has not changed. As Q-vector spaces, however, Q and Q² have dimensions one and two, so a mere enumeration is not a Q-linear isomorphism.',
 'The proposed sheets can be used as pictures, but algebraic and geometric dimension must be stated separately.',
 'This does not assign physical existence to an abstract set or resolve the philosophical status of mental representations. It answers the mathematical embedding and coordinate question only.',
 'Choose a declared equivalence relation—set bijection, linear isomorphism, homeomorphism or isometry—and test the proposed identification under that relation.',
 [('Milne algebraic geometry and rational parametrizations','https://www.jmilne.org/math/CourseNotes/AG.pdf')])

E('2024-06-05','abstraction','U','Analysis and geometry','A real constant and a geometric shape require different definitions',
 'How is e defined as a number, and what equivalence relation turns different triangles into the same geometric form?',
 'The user contrasted an ideal circle, mathematical abstraction, e and triangleness. A mathematical answer should define the objects and prove a property of each rather than infer their ontological status. An irrationality proof for e and an affine classification of triangles provide concrete content.',
 ['Define e=Σn≥0 1/n!, a convergent real series. A nondegenerate triangle is the convex hull of three noncollinear plane points.', 'Two ordered triangles are affinely equivalent when one invertible affine map sends their three vertices in the prescribed order.'],
 'The number e is irrational. Any two ordered nondegenerate plane triangles are related by exactly one invertible affine map, although their Euclidean lengths and angles need not agree.',
 'Suppose e=a/b. Choose n≥max(b,2). Then n!e and n!Σk=0^n1/k! are integers. Their difference R=Σj≥1 1/[(n+1)…(n+j)] is positive and is strictly less than Σj≥1(n+1)^(−j)=1/n<1, an impossibility for an integer. For triangles, let P=[p₂−p₁,p₃−p₁] and Q=[q₂−q₁,q₃−q₁]. Noncollinearity makes both invertible. The unique map is x↦q₁+QP⁻¹(x−p₁). Any affine map agreeing at the three vertices must have this same linear part and translation.',
 'Map the standard triangle with vertices (0,0),(1,0),(0,1) to (1,1),(3,1),(1,4).',
 'The map is (x,y)↦(1+2x,1+3y). Areas scale by |det diag(2,3)|=6, while the object remains in the same affine equivalence class of nondegenerate triangles. A triangle boundary is homeomorphic to a circle, but the filled triangle is a disk; the intended shape therefore also depends on whether its interior is included. These definitions are unlike the scalar e, for which equality is ordinary real-number equality.',
 'Numbers, embedded figures and equivalence classes are all mathematical objects, but their properties are tested by different definitions and theorems.',
 'The calculation does not settle whether an ideal object exists physically or where a mental representation is located. It also proves irrationality, not the deeper transcendence of e.',
 'State which geometric invariant is being compared with a number—area, angle, dimension or a moduli coordinate—before proposing an equation between them.',
 [('Hatcher topology of disks and circles','https://pi.math.cornell.edu/~hatcher/AT/ATchapters.html')])

E('2024-06-05','coordinate ring','A','Algebraic geometry','The circle is irreducible and its radius scaling has a contravariant map',
 'Which prime ideal defines the circle, and how does a geometric scaling act on its coordinate ring?',
 'The historical answer invoked circle coordinate rings, prime ideals and scaling. A point prime is not the same thing as an irreducible component, and a map of spaces acts in the opposite direction on coordinate functions. Both distinctions can be checked in this small example.',
 ['Let A=R[x,y]/(x²+y²−1) and B=R[u,v]/(u²+v²−4).', 'A geometric scaling φ:(x,y)↦(2x,2y) induces φ*:B→A by substituting u↦2x and v↦2y.'],
 'The defining polynomial is irreducible over R and generates a prime ideal, so A is a domain. The scaling φ is an algebraic isomorphism with inverse division by two, and its pullback is well defined. The point ideal (x−1,y) in A is maximal but does not define an extra irreducible component.',
 'If x²+y²−1 factored nontrivially over R, its degree two would force two affine-linear factors. Their highest homogeneous parts would multiply to x²+y², which has no real linear factor: a nonzero real direction cannot make a sum of two squares vanish identically along a line. Thus the polynomial is irreducible. R[x,y] is a unique factorization domain, where irreducibles are prime, proving the domain assertion. Substituting into B’s relation gives 4x²+4y²−4=0 in A; substituting x↦u/2,y↦v/2 gives the inverse homomorphism. The quotient of A by (x−1,y) is R, proving maximality.',
 'Evaluate a coordinate function f(u,v)=u+v on the radius-two point φ(3/5,4/5).',
 'The geometric point is (6/5,8/5) and f takes value14/5. The pulled-back function φ*f=2x+2y takes the same value14/5 at (3/5,4/5). This illustrates the reversed arrow B→A. Meanwhile the zero ideal of the domain A describes its single generic irreducible component; individual point ideals provide locations within that component.',
 'Coordinate rings make maps and component claims exact when the relevant ideal and arrow direction are stated.',
 'Over another field, irreducibility and point interpretation must be reconsidered. Even over R, not every maximal ideal of a finitely generated algebra need have real residue field, so this example is not a classification of every prime.',
 'Use the rational parametrization on a suitable open subset to describe the localized coordinate-ring map and its missing point.',
 [('Milne Algebraic Geometry coordinate rings','https://www.jmilne.org/math/CourseNotes/AG.pdf')])

E('2024-06-05','x^4','A','Galois theory','The recovered quartic has Klein-four rather than dihedral Galois group',
 'What are the actual roots and splitting field of x⁴−2x²+9, and which automorphisms fix each quadratic subfield?',
 'The original assistant claimed real-radical roots ±√(1±√8) and Galois group D₄. Both can be tested exactly. The corrected example is especially useful because all roots already lie in a biquadratic field of degree four.',
 ['Let α=√2+i and β=√2−i. Then αβ=3, α²=1+2i√2 and β²=1−2i√2.', 'Let K=Q(√2,i). Its two sign changes σ:√2↦−√2 and τ:i↦−i commute.'],
 'The roots are α,−α,β,−β; the splitting field is K and Gal(K/Q)≅C₂×C₂. Its three quadratic fixed fields are Q(i), Q(√2) and Q(√−2), corresponding to σ,τ and στ.',
 'The product (x²−α²)(x²−β²) equals x⁴−(α²+β²)x²+α²β²=x⁴−2x²+9. Since Q(√2) is real it cannot contain i, so [K:Q]=4. Conversely 3/α=β, and α+3/α=2√2 while α−3/α=2i. Therefore Q(α)=K, proving that this quartic is irreducible and that its splitting field has degree four, not eight. The two independent sign changes give four automorphisms and every nonidentity has order two. Their fixed quadratic combinations are immediate: στ fixes i√2, whose square is −2.',
 'Track the root permutations under σ and τ.',
 'The map τ swaps α with β and −α with −β. The map σ sends α to −β and β to −α. Their product sends each root to its negative. None has order four, unlike a rotation in D₄. Substituting the historically stated squared roots would instead use 1±√8, whereas the quadratic equation for x² is y²−2y+9=0 with discriminant −32; the missing imaginary factor explains the failed root formula.',
 'An explicit root expression, field-degree calculation and automorphism action replace the incorrect label with a complete exact classification.',
 'This is one special irreducible quartic. A generic quartic with another coefficient pattern may genuinely have D₄ or S₄ Galois group; no general quartic classification is inferred from this example.',
 'Vary the constant coefficient and classify when a reciprocal-type quartic produces a biquadratic splitting field, recording reducible and inseparable cases separately.',
 [('J S Milne Fields and Galois Theory','https://jmilne.org/math/CourseNotes/FT.pdf')])

E('2024-06-05','Hecke','A','Modular forms','The weight-dependent Hecke formula and an exact Eisenstein eigencheck',
 'Which weight and index convention determine T₂, and does the supplied q-series satisfy the resulting eigenvalue relation?',
 'The recovered formula used a₂n+2a_n without a weight or divisibility condition. In the standard level-one weight-k normalization, the second coefficient term is 2^(k−1)a_(n/2), with zero when n is odd. An Eisenstein series lets every coefficient be checked arithmetically.',
 ['Let f(τ)=Σn≥0a_n qⁿ be a weight-k modular form for SL₂(Z). For T₂ use b_n=a_(2n)+2^(k−1)a_(n/2), with a_(n/2)=0 for odd n and b₀=(1+2^(k−1))a₀.', 'Take E₄=1+240Σn≥1σ₃(n)qⁿ, where σ₃(n)=Σd|n d³.'],
 'T₂E₄=9E₄ coefficientwise. The normalized nonconstant Dirichlet series Σσ₃(n)n^(−s) equals ζ(s)ζ(s−3) for Re s>4, with local factor [(1−p^(−s))(1−p^(3−s))]⁻¹.',
 'Write n=2^r m with m odd. Divisor multiplicativity gives σ₃(n)=(1+8+⋯+8^r)σ₃(m). For r=0, σ₃(2n)=9σ₃(n). For r≥1, the finite geometric sums satisfy g_(r+1)+8g_(r−1)=9g_r, proving the Hecke relation at every positive coefficient; the constant coefficient also multiplies by nine. For the Dirichlet series, expand σ₃(n)=Σab=n b³ and interchange absolutely convergent sums when Re s>4, obtaining Σa a^(−s)Σb b^(3−s). Euler products of the zeta functions give the stated local factor.',
 'Use a₁=240,a₂=2160,a₃=6720,a₄=17520.',
 'The first transformed coefficients are b₁=a₂=2160=9a₁ and b₂=a₄+8a₁=17520+1920=19440=9a₂. The historical expression a₂n+2a_n gives a different result and even places its second term at the wrong index. The series for E₄ itself has coefficients240σ₃(n), so its unnormalized L-series includes an overall240; normalizing the first nonconstant coefficient removes that factor.',
 'The operator, modular weight and L-series normalization must be specified together. A short exact coefficient calculation can reject an incorrect Hecke formula.',
 'The coefficient identity here is for the declared level-one normalization. Other conventions and levels modify formulas, and an Eisenstein product is not the same as the degree-two local factor of an elliptic cusp form.',
 'Apply the same test to an explicitly supplied cusp-form expansion, using enough certified coefficients and a stated modular-form theorem to upgrade a finite comparison to an identity.',
 [('Milne Modular Functions and Modular Forms','https://www.jmilne.org/math/CourseNotes/MF.pdf')])

E('2024-06-05','sheaf','A','Sheaf cohomology','A nonzero H¹ from two arcs without violating sheaf gluing',
 'Which sheaf and cover define the claimed obstruction, and what exactly fails to glue?',
 'The historical statement called H¹(M) an obstruction to a global flow without naming a sheaf or complex. A two-arc cover of the circle gives an exact nonzero cohomology calculation and explains why this does not contradict the ordinary sheaf axiom for compatible sections.',
 ['Cover S¹ by connected contractible arcs U,V whose intersection has two connected components. Let R_const be the sheaf of locally constant real functions.', 'The Cech cochain spaces are C⁰=R² and C¹=R², with differential d(a,b)=(b−a,b−a); there is no triple intersection term for this two-set cover.'],
 'The Cech groups are H⁰≅R and H¹≅R²/diag(R)≅R. In particular, the overlap cocycle (1,0) is not a coboundary. The calculation agrees with sheaf cohomology because the cover and its finite intersections are acyclic for this sheaf.',
 'A locally constant section on each connected arc is one scalar; on the disconnected overlap it is two independently chosen scalars. Restriction of the two arc values gives the same difference b−a on both overlap components. Thus the kernel of d consists of equal pairs, giving H⁰=R. Its image is the diagonal line, and quotienting R² by that line leaves one dimension, detected by the difference of the two coordinates. The pair (1,0) has unequal coordinates and cannot arise from one b−a. The acyclic-cover comparison theorem supplies the identification with sheaf H¹; the finite complex itself can be computed without that general theorem.',
 'Try to solve b−a=1 on one overlap component and b−a=0 on the other.',
 'No pair of constants a,b can satisfy both equations, which is exactly the nontrivial cocycle. By contrast, if two actual local sections agree on their overlap, then a=b and they glue uniquely to one constant section on S¹, just as the sheaf axiom requires. H¹ concerns gluing data such as torsor transitions, not a failure of the axiom for compatible ordinary functions.',
 'A cohomological obstruction is meaningful only after the sheaf, cover and target gluing problem have been specified.',
 'This is an ordinary topological site, not a construction of every Grothendieck topology or an etale-flow obstruction. No dynamics is inferred from the isolated notation H¹(M).',
 'Define the desired local differential equation or torsor and identify its coefficient sheaf before interpreting its Cech cocycle as a dynamical obstruction.',
 [('Milne Lectures on Etale Cohomology sheaves and Cech constructions','https://www.jmilne.org/math/CourseNotes/LEC210.pdf')])

E('2024-06-05','SU_q','A','Quantum algebra','A braid R-matrix with a verified Hecke relation',
 'Which actual matrix satisfies the stated Yang–Baxter relation, and what further data are needed before calling it a compact quantum group?',
 'The recovered passage wrote a quantum SU_q(2) relation but did not return its Hopf-algebra or star conventions. An explicit fundamental Hecke-type braid matrix gives a complete finite algebraic calculation while keeping that missing structure visible.',
 ['For q≠0 set a=q−q⁻¹ and use the ordered basis00,01,10,11. Define Rcheck by 00↦q00, 11↦q11, 01↦10 and 10↦01+a10.', 'On three tensor factors, Rcheck₁₂=Rcheck⊗I and Rcheck₂₃=I⊗Rcheck. The braid equation is Rcheck₁₂Rcheck₂₃Rcheck₁₂=Rcheck₂₃Rcheck₁₂Rcheck₂₃.'],
 'The displayed matrix satisfies (Rcheck−qI)(Rcheck+q⁻¹I)=0 and the braid equation as identities in Laurent polynomials in q. With P the tensor swap and R=P Rcheck, this is equivalent to R₁₂R₁₃R₂₃=R₂₃R₁₃R₁₂.',
 'The 00 and11 basis states satisfy the quadratic relation immediately. On span{01,10}, the matrix is [[0,1],[1,a]], whose square is a times the matrix plus I; since a=q−q⁻¹, this is the same Hecke polynomial. For the braid equation, apply both compositions to the eight binary triples. The all-equal triples give q³ times themselves. The remaining triples reduce using a²+1=aq+q⁻², or equivalently a(q−a)=1−q⁻², after collecting coefficients; direct Laurent-polynomial multiplication verifies all eight without specializing q. Tensor swaps move factors13 into the corresponding adjacent braid positions, yielding the unbraided form.',
 'Specialize to q=1/2, so a=−3/2.',
 'All matrix entries are rational, the eigenvalues are1/2 and−2, and exact rational multiplication verifies the two eight-by-eight braid products. The resulting matrix is not unitary in the ordinary Euclidean inner product; an algebraic solution of the Yang–Baxter equation is not automatically a physical quantum gate. At q=1 it reduces to the ordinary swap, recovering the symmetric tensor permutation.',
 'An explicit R-matrix supplies a checkable braid representation. It does not by itself define all the multiplication, coproduct, antipode and star data of SU_q(2).',
 'The proof and verifier concern the chosen algebraic matrix convention. Other normalizations, spectral-parameter matrices and compact-real-form conventions must be stated separately.',
 'Add a specified quantum-group action and verify that the braid matrix intertwines its tensor action; retain the star/unitarity question as a separate condition.',
 [('Bytsko On higher spin Uq(sl2)-invariant R-matrices','https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0412482')])

E('2024-06-05','trefoil','A','Knot theory','A conventioned trefoil Jones polynomial from the bracket algebra',
 'Which crossing, writhe and variable conventions produce the recovered trefoil polynomial?',
 'The historical q+q³−q⁴ value is compatible with a standard convention, but a polynomial without normalization does not identify which mirror or variable substitution is intended. A two-strand bracket calculation fixes those choices explicitly.',
 ['Use the Temperley–Lieb algebra on two strands with basis I,e, relation e²=δe and δ=−A²−A⁻². Take the positive braid generator g=A I+A⁻¹e.', 'The closure bracket has tr(I)=δ and tr(e)=1. For writhe w use f_D(A)=(−A³)^(−w)⟨D⟩ and set t=A⁻⁴.'],
 'The closure of g³ has bracket −A⁵−A⁻³+A⁻⁷. With w=3 its normalized polynomial is A⁻⁴+A⁻¹²−A⁻¹⁶=t+t³−t⁴. Mirroring replaces t by t⁻¹.',
 'Expand g³ using e²=δe: g³=A³I+(3A+3A⁻¹δ+A⁻³δ²)e. Apply the closure trace to get A³δ+3A+3A⁻¹δ+A⁻³δ². Substituting δ and collecting Laurent powers yields −A⁵−A⁻³+A⁻⁷. The normalization factor for w=3 is −A⁻⁹, producing the stated polynomial. This is a finite exact algebra calculation. Invariance under Reidemeister moves is the separate bracket theorem: the unnormalized bracket handles movesII andIII, and the writhe factor compensates moveI.',
 'Compare the displayed positive three-crossing closure with its mirror.',
 'The chosen convention gives V(t)=t+t³−t⁴ and mirror polynomial t⁻¹+t⁻³−t⁻⁴. Both evaluate to1 at t=1, but their exponent supports differ. Changing crossing or A conventions can exchange these formulas, which explains why a recovered bare polynomial should not be declared right- or left-handed without the convention. The verifier performs the Laurent multiplication and writhe shift exactly.',
 'The recovered trefoil value is now supported by a complete calculation rather than a convention-free assertion.',
 'This does not make the Jones polynomial a complete knot invariant. Equal values in a normalization are not in general a proof of isotopy, and a framing-dependent bracket must not be confused with its normalized ambient-isotopy invariant.',
 'Implement the same algebra for a declared small braid family and check mirror substitution and normalization on each closure.',
 [('Louis Kauffman State models and the Jones polynomial','https://doi.org/10.1016/0040-9383(87)90009-7')])

E('2024-06-05','Christoffel','A','Differential geometry','Hyperbolic curvature and geodesics in a declared metric',
 'Which metric determines the Christoffel symbols, curvature and geodesics, and how are the disk and half-plane models related?',
 'The recovered passage discussed Riemannian geometry, geodesics and hyperbolic models. Specifying the metric makes these claims testable. The following calculations use the upper half-plane, give its curvature, and check an actual geodesic rather than inferring geometry from a picture.',
 ['Let H={x+iy:y>0} with metric ds²=(dx²+dy²)/y². Use the Levi-Civita connection of this metric.', 'For a conformal metric e^(2u)(dx²+dy²), Gaussian curvature is K=−e^(−2u)(u_xx+u_yy). A geodesic is affinely parametrized when its covariant acceleration vanishes.'],
 'For the stated metric, the only nonzero Christoffel symbols are Γˣ_xy=Γˣ_yx=−1/y, Γʸ_xx=1/y and Γʸ_yy=−1/y. Its curvature is −1. Vertical lines x=x₀, y=y₀e^t are unit-speed geodesics. The Cayley map w=(z−i)/(z+i) identifies this metric with 4|dw|²/(1−|w|²)² on the unit disk.',
 'The Christoffel formula Γᵏ_ij=(1/2)g^(kℓ)(∂ig_jℓ+∂jg_iℓ−∂ℓg_ij) uses g_xx=g_yy=y⁻² and inverse y²I. Differentiating yields the displayed coefficients. Put u=−log y: u_xx=0 and u_yy=y⁻², so K=−y²y⁻²=−1. For the vertical curve, ẋ=ẍ=0 and ẏ=ÿ=y, so its second geodesic equation ÿ+(ẋ²−ẏ²)/y=0 holds; its speed squared is ẏ²/y²=1. Finally |dw|²=4|dz|²/|z+i|⁴ and 1−|w|²=4y/|z+i|². Substitution proves equality of the two metrics, including their scale.',
 'Find the hyperbolic distance along a vertical segment from iy₁ to iy₂.',
 'Its length is the integral of |dy|/y, giving |log(y₂/y₁)|. A general curve joining these endpoints has length at least ∫|dy|/y≥|log(y₂/y₁)|, so the vertical segment attains the minimum. In particular i and ie² are distance2 apart although their Euclidean separation is e²−1. The exponential parametrization is therefore a consequence of the metric rather than an arbitrary time redefinition.',
 'Metric, connection, curvature and distance agree in one explicit example. This fixes the normalization that an isolated claim of negative curvature leaves unspecified.',
 'Curvature sign conventions for the Riemann tensor can differ, but Gaussian curvature of this declared metric is −1 in the standard sectional-curvature convention. No application to neural or physical spacetime is inferred.',
 'Use a real Möbius transformation to obtain the remaining geodesics as semicircles perpendicular to the boundary and transport the length calculation by isometry.',
 [('John Lee Introduction to Riemannian Manifolds, author page','https://sites.math.washington.edu/~lee/Books/RM/')])

E('2024-06-05','Virasoro','A','Lie algebras','Witt brackets and the Virasoro central term',
 'Which part of the recovered symmetry is a vector-field bracket, and what additional cocycle defines its central extension?',
 'The Virasoro and Ward-identity fragment invokes an infinite-dimensional algebra. Its concrete content can be developed without claiming a full conformal field theory: compute the vector-field bracket, specify the central cocycle, and prove the Jacobi identity for that extension.',
 ['On Laurent polynomials let ℓ_m=−z^(m+1)d/dz for m∈Z. The Witt bracket is the commutator of derivations.', 'Introduce a central symbol C, with [C,L_m]=0, and set [L_m,L_n]=(m−n)L_(m+n)+((m³−m)/12)δ_(m+n,0)C. In a representation C may act by a scalar c.'],
 'These brackets define the Virasoro Lie algebra. The span of L_−1,L_0,L_1 is an sl₂ subalgebra with no central contribution, and the noncentral quotient is the Witt algebra.',
 'Apply the commutator of ℓ_m and ℓ_n to z^r. The two second-derivative contributions cancel, leaving (m−n)ℓ_(m+n)z^r. This proves the Witt bracket and Jacobi follows from the commutator algebra of linear operators. For the central extension it remains to check the central part of Jacobi. It vanishes unless m+n+p=0. When that sum is zero, its coefficient, up to the factor1/12, is the cyclic sum of (m−n)((m+n)³−(m+n)). Substitute p=−m−n and expand: the cubic and linear terms cancel separately. Antisymmetry follows because m³−m is odd when n=−m. Thus the displayed extension is a Lie algebra. For m=−1,0,1 the polynomial m³−m is zero, so their brackets close without C.',
 'Compute two brackets that distinguish the projective modes from the full extension.',
 '[L₁,L_−1]=2L₀, while [L₂,L_−2]=4L₀+C/2. Choosing E=L₁, F=−L_−1 and H=−2L₀ gives [H,E]=2E, [H,F]=−2F and [E,F]=H. Thus a finite sl₂ subalgebra is visible, but it does not remove the independent central direction detected by modes±2. The verifier checks the cocycle cancellation on a finite range as an arithmetic check; the polynomial calculation supplies the general proof.',
 'An explicitly normalized central extension replaces the vague assertion that conformal symmetry automatically supplies a particular physical model.',
 'The mode algebra alone supplies no Hilbert space, positive-energy representation, operator domains or stress-tensor correlators. Ward identities require those further data. The central element is algebraic and is not automatically a measured central charge.',
 'Specify a highest-weight module, its vector L₀-eigenvalue and the action of C, then calculate low-level Gram matrices before making positivity claims.',
 [('David Tong String Theory, conformal field theory chapter','https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/string/string4.pdf')])

E('2024-06-06','fundamental','U','Algebraic topology','Fundamental groups distinguish boundaries, fillings and quotients',
 'Which space is intended by a sphere, triangle or torus, and which loops become contractible within that space?',
 'The recovered user questions explicitly asked about circle windings, spheres of different dimensions and nonspherical shapes. The space must be stated before its fundamental group: a filled triangle and its boundary have different answers, and an embedded picture does not determine a quotient surface.',
 ['Sⁿ={x∈R^(n+1):|x|=1}; S⁰ consists of two points. Fundamental groups are based in a chosen path component.', 'Write [a,b]=aba⁻¹b⁻¹. The torus is R²/Z²; RP² identifies antipodal points of S². A filled triangle includes its interior, while its boundary is a polygonal loop.'],
 'π₁(S¹)=Z and π₁(Sⁿ)=0 for n≥2. The torus has π₁=Z²; π₁(RP²)=Z/2; the Klein bottle has presentation ⟨a,b|aba⁻¹=b⁻¹⟩; and an oriented genus-two surface has presentation ⟨a,b,c,d|[a,b][c,d]=1⟩. A triangle boundary has group Z, whereas a filled triangle has trivial group.',
 'Lift a based circle loop through t↦e^(2πit). Its endpoint is an integer, invariant under based homotopy; concatenation adds endpoints. A loop with endpoint zero contracts by interpolating its real lift, proving the circle result. For n≥2, cellular approximation deforms a loop in the usual CW structure of Sⁿ, with one zero-cell and one n-cell, into its one-skeleton, which is a point. The quotient R²→T² is a simply connected covering with deck group Z²; S²→RP² similarly has deck group Z/2. Cutting the Klein bottle and genus-two surface into their standard polygons and applying van Kampen gives the displayed edge-word relations. Finally a triangle boundary is homeomorphic to S¹, while straight-line contraction to an interior point stays inside the convex filled triangle.',
 'Compare a once-around triangular boundary loop with the same path allowed to move through its interior.',
 'Within the boundary the winding class is1 and cannot contract. In the filled triangle, H(s,t)=(1−s)γ(t)+s v contracts the loop to the base vertex v, fixes its endpoints and remains in the convex triangle. For the torus, the loop t↦(2t,3t) modZ² has class(2,3); it is noncontractible despite ending where it starts. The Klein relation conjugates b to b⁻¹ rather than commuting a and b, so its group must not be replaced by Z².',
 'The shape questions become precise through loop homotopy, explicit coverings and declared identifications.',
 'The presentations are classical theorems, not results inferred from numerical graph cycles. S⁰ has trivial fundamental group at either point but is disconnected. Higher homotopy groups of spheres are a different, substantially richer subject.',
 'For a chosen cell complex, state its attaching maps and use van Kampen before computing its abelianization; the latter gives H₁ and can lose noncommutative information.',
 [('Allen Hatcher Algebraic Topology, fundamental groups','https://pi.math.cornell.edu/~hatcher/AT/ATchapters.html')])

E('2024-06-06','wedge','A','Exterior calculus','Wedge products, Hodge duality and a genuine closed-form obstruction',
 'Which degrees, orientation and metric define the exterior operations, and when does a closed form have a global potential?',
 'The recovered exterior-calculus passages mixed antisymmetry, volume, differential forms and topology. The key distinctions are degree, closure and exactness. A disk example verifies Stokes directly, while a punctured-plane example shows precisely why local potentials need not glue into one global scalar.',
 ['For a p-form α and q-form β, α∧β=(−1)^(pq)β∧α. Thus two one-forms anticommute, but two two-forms commute.', 'On oriented Euclidean R³ use dx∧dy∧dz as volume and the Hodge star defined by α∧*β=⟨α,β⟩vol. In particular *(dx∧dy)=dz.'],
 'On the plane, η=(−y dx+x dy)/2 satisfies dη=dx∧dy and ∮_(unit circle)η=π. On the punctured plane, ω=(−y dx+x dy)/(x²+y²) is closed but not exact; its circle integral is2π. This is a well-defined topological obstruction to one global potential.',
 'Differentiate η term by term: d(−y dx)=−dy∧dx=dx∧dy and d(x dy)=dx∧dy, giving dη=dx∧dy. Parameterize the circle by x=cos t,y=sin t to obtain η=dt/2 and integralπ, equal to the disk area. For ω, the coordinate calculation ∂x[x/(x²+y²)]−∂y[−y/(x²+y²)]=0 gives dω=0 wherever the denominator is nonzero. On the unit circle ω=dt and its integral is2π. If ω=df globally, the integral along any closed parametrized path would equal f at the endpoint minus f at the start, hence zero. The nonzero period disproves exactness. Locally one can use an angle branch, but no single-valued angle exists on the full punctured plane.',
 'Express area and volume using coordinate differentials rather than informal antisymmetric symbols.',
 '(a dx+b dy)∧(c dx+d dy)=(ad−bc)dx∧dy, so the coefficient is the oriented parallelogram area. In R³, (dx∧dy)∧dz is the volume form and its coefficient changes sign when orientation reverses. A scalar density ρ becomes a mass three-form ρ dx∧dy∧dz; multiplying scalar functions alone does not create a three-form. The Hodge star additionally depends on the metric, whereas wedge multiplication does not.',
 'The local operations and the global obstruction are now separate statements with direct calculations.',
 'The closed-not-exact example concerns the punctured domain; including the origin makes ω undefined. Hodge theory on general manifolds requires boundary conditions and analytic hypotheses not supplied by these identities.',
 'Compute periods on a declared basis of H₁ of a domain and test whether a closed one-form can be exact, rather than using an undefined global-flow obstruction.',
 [('Bott and Tu Differential Forms in Algebraic Topology, publisher record','https://link.springer.com/book/10.1007/978-1-4757-3951-0')])

E('2024-06-06','density','A','Computational geometry','Affine density integrates exactly on a simplex',
 'Which basis represents the density, which region is integrated, and when is an implicit level set a regular surface?',
 'The recovered density, implicit-surface and finite-element discussion becomes concrete on one tetrahedron. Barycentric coordinates give an exact mass formula and an explicit positivity criterion; the regular-value test prevents a singular point from being mislabeled a smooth surface.',
 ['Let T be the tetrahedron with vertices v₀=(0,0,0), v₁=(1,0,0), v₂=(0,1,0), v₃=(0,0,1). Its barycentric coordinates are λ₀=1−x−y−z, λ₁=x, λ₂=y, λ₃=z.', 'An affine density is ρ=Σ_(i=0)^3 ρ_i λ_i. A level set F=0 is regular at a point when ∇F is nonzero there.'],
 'For every affine density on a nondegenerate tetrahedron, ∫_Tρ dV=Vol(T)(ρ₀+ρ₁+ρ₂+ρ₃)/4. It is nonnegative everywhere if and only if every vertex value is nonnegative. For F=x²+y²+z²−r², the zero set is a regular two-dimensional surface when r>0, but at r=0 it is one singular point.',
 'Affine permutations of the four vertices preserve the tetrahedron and its volume measure. They permute the barycentric functions, so all four integrals ∫λ_i are equal. Since Σλ_i=1, each is Vol(T)/4. Integrating the linear combination proves the mass formula. Since λ_i≥0 and sum to one, ρ is a convex combination of its vertex values; their nonnegativity implies pointwise nonnegativity. The converse follows by evaluating at a vertex. For the implicit surface, ∇F=2(x,y,z). On F=0 with r>0 its norm is2r, nonzero, so the implicit-function theorem gives a local surface chart. With r=0 the only real solution is the origin, where the gradient vanishes and there is no two-dimensional neighborhood.',
 'Integrate ρ=1+x+y+z on the standard tetrahedron.',
 'The volume is1/6, and the four vertex values are1,2,2,2. Consequently mass=(1/6)(7/4)=7/24. Direct integration gives the same answer: ∫1=1/6 and ∫x=∫y=∫z=1/24. The mean density is7/4, equal to the value at the centroid(1/4,1/4,1/4). Nonnegative nodal coefficients preserve positivity for this affine basis, a property that need not survive an arbitrary oscillatory higher-order basis.',
 'The density representation now yields a finite exact calculation rather than an unspecified numerical pipeline.',
 'This is exact for affine densities on straight nondegenerate simplices. Curved elements, nonlinear densities and signed Jacobians need additional quadrature and geometry analysis. The regularity test does not assert that every level set is globally connected or sphere-like.',
 'For a declared quadratic density use simplex monomial integrals, then compare its exact integral with a stated quadrature rule and check whether positivity remains preserved.',
 [('Long Chen Introduction to Finite Element Methods','https://www.math.uci.edu/~chenlong/226/Ch2FEM.pdf')])

E('2024-06-06','PCG','A','Numerical linear algebra','The SPD conditions behind conjugate gradients and multigrid',
 'Which discrete operator and preconditioner make conjugate gradients valid, and what improvement can actually be certified?',
 'The recovered computational passage named PCG and algebraic multigrid without a matrix or convergence conditions. A three-variable Poisson problem supplies a complete calculation. It also shows why simply labeling a scaling operation a preconditioner does not demonstrate faster convergence.',
 ['Let A be the3×3 tridiagonal matrix with diagonal2 and adjacent off-diagonal−1. A symmetric positive-definite matrix M defines the symmetric preconditioned operator M^(−1/2)AM^(−1/2).', 'For an SPD operator with condition number κ, the classical CG energy-error bound after k steps is at most2[(√κ−1)/(√κ+1)]^k times the initial energy error.'],
 'A is SPD with eigenvalues2−√2,2,2+√2, condition number3+2√2, and A⁻¹(1,1,1)ᵀ=(3/2,2,3/2)ᵀ. The Jacobi preconditioner M=2I leaves the condition number unchanged. An AMG cycle used inside ordinary PCG must supply a fixed symmetric positive-definite preconditioning action to retain the standard theory.',
 'For x=(x₁,x₂,x₃), xᵀAx=x₁²+(x₂−x₁)²+(x₃−x₂)²+x₃². This is positive for every nonzero vector, proving SPD without numerical eigenvalue tolerances. The sine vectors x_j=sin(jkπ/4), k=1,2,3 satisfy the difference equation with eigenvalue2−2cos(kπ/4). Their ratio gives κ=(2+√2)/(2−√2)=3+2√2. Multiplying the proposed solution by A gives(1,1,1). For M=2I the preconditioned eigenvalues are exactly half the original values, so their ratio is unchanged. Standard CG orthogonalizes residuals in the appropriate Krylov space; in exact arithmetic it terminates within the number of distinct eigenvalues, but a nonsymmetric or varying preconditioner invalidates this particular argument.',
 'Run unpreconditioned CG from zero with b=(1,1,1).',
 'The first step has α₀=(bᵀb)/(bᵀAb)=3/2, x₁=(3/2,3/2,3/2) and residual r₁=(−1/2,1,−1/2). Its squared norm is3/2, so β₀=1/2 and p₁=r₁+(1/2)b=(0,3/2,0). Then α₁=(3/2)/(9/2)=1/3, giving x₂=(3/2,2,3/2) and zero residual. Symmetry of this right-hand side reduced the active spectral support to two modes, so convergence occurs in two steps rather than the general three-step bound.',
 'Both the operator hypotheses and the actual finite iteration are explicit. The preconditioner claim is assessable through its spectrum and symmetry.',
 'Finite precision, nonlinear or changing AMG cycles and indefinite PDE discretizations need separate methods and stopping analysis. The ideal M=A gives condition number one but solving with M may cost as much as the original problem.',
 'Specify one concrete symmetric multigrid cycle and prove positive definiteness of its action before comparing measured iterations on larger mesh families.',
 [('Yousef Saad Iterative Methods for Sparse Linear Systems, author-hosted text','https://www-users.cse.umn.edu/~saad/IterMethBook_2ndEd.pdf')])

E(None,'Tonnetz','T','Discrete and smooth topology','A toroidal toy Tonnetz separates graph cycles from surface homology',
 'How do Hamiltonian graph cycles, surface homology and Morse critical points differ on a precisely defined toroidal example?',
 'A retrieved document fragment mentions Hamiltonian cycles, Morse theory and a toroidal Tonnetz, but its underlying mathematical document and exact original date were not recovered. The following independently constructed model develops those themes without representing it as a transcription or an established musical mapping.',
 ['Take vertices(i,j)∈Z/3×Z/4. Triangulate each periodic square with edges in directions(1,0),(0,1),(1,1). This is a simplicial triangulation of a torus.', 'A Hamiltonian cycle visits each graph vertex once. Betti numbers of the filled simplicial surface use both edges and faces. On the smooth torus set f(u,v)=cos u+2cos v.'],
 'The complex has12 vertices,36 edges and24 triangular faces, with Betti numbers(1,2,1) over F₂. Its one-skeleton alone has first Betti number25. The graph contains a Hamiltonian cycle. The smooth function f has one minimum, two saddles and one maximum, satisfying1−2+1=0.',
 'The periodic rectangular quotient is a torus: gluing opposite sides of a rectangle gives the standard surface, and the chosen diagonal triangulates it. Each of12 vertices contributes one edge in each of three distinct directions, yielding36 edges, and each of12 squares has two triangles, yielding24 faces. Connectedness gives b₀=1. A connected closed triangulated surface has one mod-two fundamental two-cycle, so b₂=1; Euler characteristic12−36+24=0 then gives b₁=2. Removing all faces leaves a connected graph with cycle rank E−V+1=25. For f, derivatives vanish when sin u=sin v=0. The four points have Hessian diag(−cos u,−2cos v), which is nondegenerate, with the stated indices. Thus the Morse alternating sum matches the surface Euler characteristic.',
 'List a Hamiltonian cycle as row-wise alternating triples, using periodic adjacency.',
 'Visit(0,0),(1,0),(2,0),(2,1),(1,1),(0,1),(0,2),(1,2),(2,2),(2,3),(1,3),(0,3), then return to(0,0). Every consecutive pair is an allowed horizontal or vertical edge, and all12 vertices occur once. This graph cycle is not a claim that a gradient trajectory of f is periodic. Indeed the negative gradient equation gives df/dt=−|∇f|², so a nonstationary periodic gradient orbit is impossible. The checker constructs both boundary matrices and obtains ranks11 and23, separately confirming b₁=2.',
 'The construction realizes all three topics while showing that their different meanings of cycle cannot be interchanged.',
 'This is a new finite mathematical example, not a recovered original Tonnetz formula or a claim about musical consonance. The original full document requested on June7 remains unrecovered.',
 'Choose a specific pitch-class labeling and define its edge rules before asking which cycles represent musical transformations; keep that added interpretation separate from the topology already proved.',
 [('Allen Hatcher Algebraic Topology, cellular homology','https://pi.math.cornell.edu/~hatcher/AT/ATchapters.html')])

E('2024-06-07','scaling','A','Probability and scaling','A renormalization map with a proved Gaussian fixed point',
 'What is the actual scaling operator, its fixed point and the domain of attraction in one rigorously defined example?',
 'The recovered scaling passage is truncated before its recurrence can be reconstructed. A complete replacement should therefore declare a new operator. Independent normalized summation gives an exact renormalization map and explains a genuine universality statement with finite-variance hypotheses.',
 ['For a centered random variable X of variance σ², let φ(t)=E exp(itX). Define the law transformation R(X)=(X₁+X₂)/√2 for independent copies.', 'On characteristic functions this is (Rφ)(t)=φ(t/√2)². A fixed point means equality of probability laws, not equality of individual sample paths.'],
 'The centered Gaussian law of variance σ² is a fixed point of R. For every centered finite-variance law, R^k(X) converges in distribution to that Gaussian. If an r-th cumulant exists, its value after k iterations is2^[k(1−r/2)] times its initial value.',
 'Independence gives the product of characteristic functions and therefore the stated operator. A Gaussian has φ(t)=exp(−σ²t²/2), and substitution leaves it unchanged. For a centered finite-variance law, differentiation at zero gives φ(s)=1−σ²s²/2+o(s²). After k iterations n=2^k independent summands are divided by√n, so φ_k(t)=φ(t/√n)^n. Taking a logarithm near1 yields nlogφ(t/√n)=−σ²t²/2+o(1), hence convergence to the Gaussian characteristic function; the continuity theorem converts it to weak convergence. For existing cumulants, logφ has coefficient κ_r(it)^r/r!, and replacing it by2logφ(t/√2) multiplies that coefficient by2^(1−r/2). This proves the cumulant statement, whose existence assumptions are stronger than needed for weak convergence.',
 'Start with a symmetric variable taking±1 with equal probability.',
 'Its characteristic function is cos t. After k steps it is [cos(t/√(2^k))]^(2^k), tending to exp(−t²/2). The variance remains1, while the fourth cumulant is initially E X⁴−3(EX²)²=−2 and becomes−2/2^k. Odd cumulants vanish by symmetry. The persistence of a fixed variance and decay of this non-Gaussian fourth cumulant demonstrate two different scaling directions explicitly.',
 'The word universality now names a proved convergence statement for a declared operation and a stated class of initial laws.',
 'Finite variance and independence matter. Heavy-tailed laws can have different normalizations and limits; correlated systems and deterministic bifurcation renormalization are not covered. The incomplete historical recurrence is not silently replaced by a claimed original formula.',
 'For a chosen dependent sequence, prove a suitable central-limit theorem or identify a counterexample before asserting that the same renormalization basin survives dependence.',
 [('MIT OpenCourseWare Probability characteristic functions and the central limit theorem','https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-175-theory-of-probability-spring-2014/')])

E('2024-06-08','antisymmetric','A','Linear algebra and differential forms','Antisymmetry does not imply a magnetic monopole',
 'What follows from skew symmetry alone, and which extra differential and topological assumptions create a monopole obstruction?',
 'The recovered antisymmetric-tensor passage associated skew symmetry with electromagnetic and monopole language. Linear algebra supplies dimension and determinant facts, while differential forms supply a separate flux obstruction. Neither should be inferred from the other without the missing equations and domain.',
 ['A real d×d skew-symmetric matrix satisfies Aᵀ=−A and has d(d−1)/2 independent entries. A two-form is F=(1/2)ΣF_ij dx_i∧dx_j.', 'An exact two-form has F=dA for a globally defined one-form A. A magnetic flux model on a sphere is F=g sinθ dθ∧dφ.'],
 'Every odd-dimensional real skew matrix has determinant zero. Antisymmetry alone imposes no equation dF=0 or d*F=0. The sphere form with g≠0 has integral4πg and cannot equal dA for one global smooth potential on S², although it has compatible local potentials on two patches.',
 'For a skew matrix detA=detAᵀ=det(−A)=(−1)^d detA. In odd dimension this forces detA=0 over R. A two-form is automatically alternating, but its coefficients may be arbitrary smooth functions; their derivatives need not satisfy closure. On S² integrate the displayed form: g∫₀²πdφ∫₀πsinθdθ=4πg. If F=dA globally, Stokes on the closed oriented sphere would give integral zero, a contradiction. Away from the south pole use A_N=g(1−cosθ)dφ, and away from the north pole use A_S=−g(1+cosθ)dφ. Direct differentiation gives F on each patch; their difference on the overlap is2g dφ. Apparent coordinate singularities are removed on the pole where the corresponding coefficient vanishes.',
 'Compare a four-dimensional skew matrix with this sphere flux.',
 'In dimension4 there are six independent entries, so skew symmetry leaves a six-parameter linear space. For example blockdiag([[0,a],[−a,0]],[[0,b],[−b,0]]) has determinant a²b² and need not be singular. In contrast, the nonzero sphere flux is a global obstruction, not a count of components. If a global potential exists on a ball containing the sphere, then its exact curvature has zero flux through that sphere; removing the center changes the domain and can permit the nonexact class.',
 'The recovered theme is repaired into independent algebraic and topological statements, with the exact missing hypotheses visible.',
 'No observed monopole or new interaction is implied. Charge quantization additionally requires a specified charged field, gauge group and transition-function condition; it does not follow from real two-form antisymmetry alone.',
 'For a U(1) bundle, impose single-valued transition functions on the overlap and derive the integral cohomology condition for its curvature in explicitly stated units.',
 [('David Tong Gauge Theory, magnetic monopoles and gauge patches','https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/gaugetheory.html')])

E('2024-06-07','Monte','A','Finite probability models','A complete two-spin Gibbs and Metropolis model',
 'Can one of the listed statistical-mechanics and Monte Carlo frameworks be made into a fully normalized finite model?',
 'The recovered June7 list names statistical mechanics, networks, Monte Carlo and spin systems without a common equation. A new two-spin example supplies one complete mathematical case. It supports no claim that all the listed continuum and chaotic models have thereby been constructed.',
 ['Take states σ=(σ₁,σ₂)∈{−1,1}² and energy E(σ)=−Jσ₁σ₂. At inverse temperature β set π(σ)=exp(−βE(σ))/Z.', 'A Metropolis step chooses one spin uniformly, proposes its flip and accepts with probability min(1,exp(−βΔE)); rejection leaves the state unchanged.'],
 'Z=4cosh(βJ), E_π[σ₁σ₂]=tanh(βJ), and the one-spin Metropolis chain is reversible with stationary law π. For βJ=log2, each aligned state has probability2/5 and each anti-aligned state probability1/10.',
 'There are two aligned states of energy−J and two anti-aligned states of energy J, so Z=2e^(βJ)+2e^(−βJ)=4cosh(βJ). Inserting the product σ₁σ₂=±1 into the expectation gives [2e^(βJ)−2e^(−βJ)]/Z=tanh(βJ). For neighboring states x,y differing by one spin, the symmetric proposal has probability1/2. Thus π(x)P(x,y)=(1/(2Z))min(e^(−βE(x)),e^(−βE(y))), visibly symmetric in x,y. Summing detailed balance over x proves stationarity. At βJ=log2, the aligned Boltzmann weight is2, the anti-aligned weight1/2, and Z=5, giving the stated probabilities.',
 'Compute all transition types at βJ=log2.',
 'From an aligned state, a proposed flip raises energy by2J and is accepted with probability e^(−2βJ)=1/4. Therefore each of its two anti-aligned neighbors has transition probability1/8 and the self-transition is3/4. From an anti-aligned state, both single-spin flips lower energy and each aligned neighbor is reached with probability1/2. Every aligned-to-anti edge has stationary flow(2/5)(1/8)=1/20, matching its reverse flow(1/10)(1/2). The correlation is(4/5)−(1/5)=3/5, agreeing with tanh(log2). The finite chain is irreducible, and its positive aligned self-transition makes it aperiodic.',
 'A complete normalizing constant, observable and invariant transition kernel replace a list of framework names with a checkable model.',
 'This is an independently authored finite example linked to recovered assistant topics. It supplies no spin-glass phase transition, fluid turbulence solver or thermodynamic-limit conclusion. A two-spin finite partition function is analytic at every finite real βJ.',
 'For a larger declared graph, write the energy and transition kernel explicitly, then distinguish exact equilibrium identities from the separate rate at which the chain approaches equilibrium.',
 [('David Tong Statistical Physics, canonical ensemble','https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/statphys.html'),('Metropolis et al Equation of state calculations by fast computing machines','https://doi.org/10.1063/1.1699114')])

E('2024-06-30','rotation','A','Quantum probability and optimization','A qubit rotation has an exact gradient and a testable learning rule',
 'Which derivative does a rotation-based output require, and is the recovered additive weight update actually gradient descent?',
 'The quantum-neuron passage supplied qubit states, controlled rotations, a measurement probability and a heuristic learning rule. A one-qubit model is sufficient to compute the correct derivative and produce a counterexample to an update that substitutes an arbitrary feature expectation for that derivative.',
 ['Let R_y(w)=[[cos(w/2),−sin(w/2)],[sin(w/2),cos(w/2)]] act on |0⟩. Measure |1⟩ with probability p(w)=sin²(w/2).', 'For target t∈[0,1], set L(w)=(1/2)(p(w)−t)². The recovered heuristic has the form w_new=w+η(t−p(w))f, where f is a feature expectation.'],
 'The exact derivatives are p′(w)=(1/2)sin w and L′(w)=(p(w)−t)(1/2)sin w. The parameter-shift identity is p′(w)=[p(w+π/2)−p(w−π/2)]/2. Replacing this derivative by a general f does not guarantee descent.',
 'Matrix multiplication gives R_y(w)|0⟩=cos(w/2)|0⟩+sin(w/2)|1⟩. Squaring the second amplitude yields p, and elementary differentiation gives p′. Equivalently p=(1−cos w)/2, so evaluating it at w±π/2 gives the parameter-shift identity directly. The chain rule yields L′. For any point with L′≠0, the exact update w−ηL′ decreases L for all sufficiently small positive η by the first-order Taylor expansion. The heuristic is the same gradient update only when f=p′(w), or under another explicitly proved equality that yields an appropriate descent direction; a feature name alone does not establish this.',
 'Choose w=3π/2, t=1, f=1 and η=1/5.',
 'Initially p=1/2 and L=1/8, while p′=−1/2 and L′=1/4. Gradient descent therefore decreases w. The heuristic instead increases w by1/10. Its new probability is [1−sin(1/10)]/2, approximately0.450083, so the new loss is approximately0.151204, strictly larger than1/8. The strict inequality follows without rounding because sin(1/10)>0. Parameter-shift evaluations still reproduce the negative derivative exactly, identifying the missing sign and factor in the heuristic.',
 'The recovered output formula now has a complete analytic training calculation and an explicit failure case for an unjustified update.',
 'This is a finite quantum-circuit probability model, not a biological-neuron claim or evidence of a quantum computational advantage. Sampling noise and multi-parameter controlled circuits require their own gradient and error analyses.',
 'For a specified circuit with one parameter per gate, derive each parameter-shift rule and compare exact loss changes with unbiased finite-shot gradient estimates.',
 [('Maria Schuld et al Evaluating analytic gradients on quantum hardware','https://arxiv.org/abs/1811.11184')])

E('2024-06-30','Lindblad','A','Quantum channels','Entanglement, dephasing and observability in a two-qubit calculation',
 'Which completely positive channel models the stated decoherence, and which observables can distinguish its effects?',
 'The recovered assistant passage mentions CNOT entanglement and Lindblad decoherence. A Bell pair undergoing local dephasing yields an exact channel, a closed-form density matrix and a precise observability distinction between individual marginals and joint correlations.',
 ['Let |Φ⁺⟩=(|00⟩+|11⟩)/√2, obtainable by a Hadamard on the first qubit followed by CNOT. Use Pauli matrices X and Z.', 'For γ≥0, take Lindblad operator L=√(γ/2)Z on the first qubit. Its generator is D(ρ)=(γ/2)(ZρZ−ρ). Set q=e^(−γt) for t≥0.'],
 'The resulting channel is E_t(ρ)=((1+q)/2)ρ+((1−q)/2)ZρZ. It is trace preserving and completely positive. Applied to the first half of |Φ⁺⟩, it leaves each one-qubit marginal I/2, but changes the joint expectation ⟨X⊗X⟩ from1 to q while ⟨Z⊗Z⟩ remains1.',
 'In the Z basis, ZρZ keeps diagonal entries and reverses off-diagonal signs. Thus the generator leaves diagonal entries fixed and gives derivative−γρ_01 for off-diagonal entries; the solution multiplies them by q. The displayed mixture has exactly that action. Its Kraus operators are K₀=√((1+q)/2)I and K₁=√((1−q)/2)Z. Both coefficients are nonnegative because0≤q≤1, and ΣK_j†K_j=I. A Kraus representation proves complete positivity, including after tensoring with an arbitrary ancilla, while the last identity proves trace preservation. For the Bell input, the only nonzero entries are the two populations1/2 and coherences q/2 linking00 and11. Partial traces remove those coherences, leaving I/2, whereas X⊗X exchanges00 and11 and therefore reads their sum q. Z⊗Z is+1 on both populated states.',
 'Compare no dephasing q=1 with partial dephasing q=1/4.',
 'Every individual computational-basis or rotated-basis measurement has the same uniform marginal in both cases. The joint X⊗X expectation changes from1 to1/4, and the eigenvalues of the two-qubit state are(1+q)/2,(1−q)/2,0,0. At q=1/4 these are5/8,3/8,0,0, explicitly positive and normalized. Complete dephasing q=0 gives the classical mixture(|00⟩⟨00|+|11⟩⟨11|)/2, while every q>0 retains nonzero coherence in this basis.',
 'The example proves a specific decoherence evolution and specifies exactly which observation is blind to its changing parameter.',
 'Lindblad form assumes a specified Markovian quantum dynamical model; it does not derive a biological mechanism. Negative time or γ<0 makes the displayed mixture coefficients unsuitable for this channel family. Marginal blindness here is not a claim that all measurements are uninformative.',
 'Specify a measurement family, calculate its probabilities under the channel and test whether its parameter map is injective before claiming decoherence is experimentally identifiable.',
 [('David Tong Advanced Quantum Mechanics, open quantum systems','https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/aqm/aqm.pdf'),('Gorini Kossakowski Sudarshan Completely positive dynamical semigroups','https://doi.org/10.1063/1.522979')])

E('2024-06-30','gyre','A','Identifiability','An additive correction ansatz does not identify a new mechanism',
 'Which independently fixed quantities make a proposed correction identifiable, and what cannot be determined from the sum alone?',
 'The historical gyre fragment adds undefined corrections to beta functions, vacuum energy, a magnetic moment and a holographic entropy. The mathematically defensible development is an identifiability test: an additive symbol is a parametrization until a model defines it and independent information separates it from its baseline.',
 ['For one scalar observable let F(θ,g)=θ+g, with both θ and g unknown real parameters. Identifiability means different admissible parameters produce different observable predictions.', 'For four independent additive observables write F(θ,g)=θ+g on R⁴×R⁴. A local sensitivity matrix is the derivative of the prediction map.'],
 'With no further constraints, the scalar sum has a one-dimensional unidentifiable direction, and the four-observable sum has a four-dimensional one. Knowing the baseline independently can identify a residual, but does not by itself explain or predict that residual.',
 'For every real t, F(θ+t,g−t)=θ+g=F(θ,g). Thus each observed sum has an entire affine line of compatible parameter pairs. The Jacobian is[1,1], whose null space is span{(1,−1)} and whose rank is1. In four dimensions the derivative is[I₄ I₄], of rank4, with kernel{(v,−v):v∈R⁴}. This exactly matches the four-parameter family of indistinguishable decompositions. If θ is fixed by an independent calibrated model, then g=F−θ is algebraically determined by the observation. But this is a fitted residual: obtaining future values of g still requires a separately defined predictive rule, and uncertainty in θ propagates directly to uncertainty in the inferred g.',
 'Take an observed value y=7 and leave both baseline and correction unconstrained.',
 '(θ,g)=(7,0),(5,2),(10,−3) all give y=7. Repeating the same scalar measurement at greater precision cannot remove this structural ambiguity. If a second observation instead has the declared law y₂=θ+2g and yields9, subtracting gives g=2 and then θ=5; the sensitivity matrix[[1,1],[1,2]] has determinant1, so it is invertible. The extra independent equation resolves the ambiguity because it has a different known dependence, not because it is labeled another correction.',
 'This is a complete finite theorem about the recovered ansatz form. It identifies the additional mathematical data needed for a testable model.',
 'No gyre field, beta-function correction, cosmological constant or new quantum-gravity interaction is derived. The four historical quantities have different dimensions and cannot be added to each other. Even the entropy expression needs its gravitational units and normalization declared.',
 'Define an explicit correction function of shared parameters and specified observables, compute its sensitivity rank, and state which parameters remain indistinguishable before attempting estimation.',
 [('Rothenberg Identification in Parametric Models','https://doi.org/10.2307/1913267')])

extra={
1:' The prime subfield of F₄ is only{0,1}; its remaining two elements solve the same irreducible quadratic over F₂. Thus field cardinality four does not mean characteristic four, and its two-dimensional vector-space structure is over F₂, not over R.',
2:' Both generators have trace zero, so both flows preserve oriented area: their determinants are always one. Area preservation therefore does not imply bounded trajectories or recurrence. This supplies an additional exact counterexample to replacing a dynamical periodicity argument by a Lie-algebra invariant.',
3:' Angular momentum in this example is another first integral but still not a canonical Casimir. For L=x₁p₂−x₂p₁ the bracket {L,x₁}=x₂ is nonzero. Conservation by the rotationally symmetric oscillator and invariance under every Hamiltonian vector field are therefore different even for this second conserved quantity.',
4:' The rational subspace topology also matters: no nonempty interval of R is countable, whereas every subset of Q is countable. Dense placement consequently does not make Q an open one-dimensional real manifold or turn a rational sheet into a continuous plane.',
5:' The inverse affine map is ((u−1)/2,(v−1)/3), proving equivalence in both directions. Its determinant is1/6, reciprocal to the forward area factor. This exact invertibility uses nondegenerate triangles; a collinear triple has no invertible two-dimensional affine identification with the standard filled triangle.',
6:' Evaluation at a real circle point is a surjective algebra map to R whose kernel is the ideal of coordinate functions vanishing there. The quotient is R, hence that ideal is maximal. This is why a point ideal and the zero ideal describing the irreducible component carry different information.',
7:' The fixed fields of the three distinct order-two automorphisms are exactly Q(√2), Q(i) and Q(√−2). For instance simultaneous sign reversal fixes the product i√2 while changing each factor. Each is a quadratic extension of Q, and the splitting field has degree two over each, exactly matching the subgroup indices.',
8:' The constant coefficient obeys the same eigenvalue law: the n=0 formula gives1+8=9. The historical alternative would instead give constant coefficient3, already contradicting that eigenvalue on E₄. For n=2^r m with m odd, σ₃(n)=σ₃(m)(1+8+⋯+8^r); the identity at2n is the finite geometric-sum recurrence. For odd n the second term vanishes. This directly accounts for every index rather than inferring modularity from a few coefficients.',
10:' The two mixed-basis eigenvalues are roots of λ²−aλ−1, namely q and−q⁻¹. Their nonzero product−1 proves invertibility for every allowed q. At q=1/2 the eigenvalue magnitude2 also rules out Euclidean unitarity immediately, since a unitary matrix can only have eigenvalues of absolute value one.',
11:' The normalization can be checked against a single positive curl: its bracket contributes−A³, while its writhe increases by one. Multiplication by(−A³)⁻¹ cancels that factor. A negative curl gives the reciprocal cancellation. This explains the role of writhe before applying the convention to the three-crossing example. The crossingless unknot has bracket and normalized value one under this same closure convention, fixing its overall scalar normalization.',
12:' The same vertical-distance proof works for arbitrary positive endpoints without a small-distance approximation. The logarithm is dimensionless because it depends on a ratio, and exchanging the endpoints changes its sign before the absolute value. This also verifies symmetry of that particular distance formula.',
17:' Positive diagonal scaling alone changes the units of eigenvalues, while the condition number measures their ratio. The example therefore separates a valid SPD preconditioner from an effective one: validity permits PCG, whereas effectiveness requires an improved spectral distribution or a cheaper total solve.',
21:' At β=0 all four states have equal probability and the spin correlation is zero. Increasing positive βJ raises the aligned probability continuously, approaching one in total as βJ tends to infinity. These are finite-state limiting statements, not a nonanalytic phase transition.',
22:' A constant feature f=1 is itself an admissible expectation, for example of the identity observable. The counterexample therefore does not depend on an impossible feature value. Its failure arises because this feature is unrelated to the derivative of the specified rotation probability.'}
